content: Rootkits are malicious software tools that allow the attacker to gain low-level access to a computer system, often to hide the presence of other malware. This can be extremely dangerous because they can prevent system administrators and security software from detecting and removing the malware. There are several types of rootkits, including kernel-mode, user-mode, and hardware rootkits. Kernel-mode rootkits are the most dangerous because they operate within the kernel, making them nearly invisible to traditional security software. One of the most well-known kernel-mode rootkits is the Rootkit Kit (Rkkit), which allows for the replacement of system binaries with modified versions. To hide malware, a kernel-mode rootkit would typically take control of the process table and memory management to intercept and prevent security software from accessing certain system calls. This makes rootkits extremely challenging to detect and remove from a system.

